Hi, I stumbled upon this RFC while looking if there was anyway to have a Next site automatically rebuild _or_ build a newly updated/added piece of content.

I am using SSG in tandem with a headless CMS like many others here, since the content rarely changes, but new pieces of content are more frequently added and so this functionality baked into Next.js would be wonderful not a showstopper at the moment for me.

**However, I was able to replicate this sort of functionality** in a hackyish way using just web hooks (available within my CMS — Craft CMS), [PM2](https://pm2.keymetrics.io/), and node's [exec](https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html#child_process_child_process_execsync_command_options) method.

The workflow is pretty simple (if you're up for having PM2 manage your Next.js process for you) and straightforward. Here's what it looked like for me, and should be as simple for you if your CMS supports it.

1. Set up an API route within your pages folder where you'll want to hit to trigger a rebuilt of your site. For example, an endpoint called `triggerNextRebuild`.

2. Add logic in your route file that'll execute your `next build` command using the `exec` command. Here's how mine looks. Once the build finishes, I update a file called `.rebuilt` with the timestamp of the latest built (this is for PM2). 
```
import { execSync } from 'child_process';
import * as fs from 'fs';
import * as path from 'path';

export default async (request, response) => {

    // Execute the command to regenerate your site
    const output = execSync(`npm run build`)
        .toString()
        .trim();

    // Once building has finished, update the .rebuilt file -- pm2 will restart due to watching .rebuilt file
    const writePath = path.join(process.cwd(), '.rebuilt');
    const now = Date.now();

    fs.writeFile(writePath, `Site rebuilt at ${now}}`, (error) => {
        if (error) console.log(error);
        console.log('File is created successfully.');
    });

    response.end(`Building has finished. File ${writePath} has been written at ${now}`);
}
```

3. Install `pm2` and have it watch that `.rebuilt` file. Configure it to restart automatically when it sees that the `.rebuilt` file was changed (which would occur at the end of the `next build` command completing).

For that, I use this command to start my Next.js process 
```
pm2 start ecosystem.config.js --no-daemon
``` 

with my `ecosystem.config.js
file being configured like this

```
module.exports = {
    apps: [{
        name: "my-app",
        script: "npm",
        args: "start",
        watch: [".rebuilt"],
        autorestart: true,
        ignoreWatch : ["node_modules"],
    }]
}
```

4. The last part is to just configure a webhook from your CMS to hit your `/api/triggerNextRebuild` endpoint after a specified action (for me, it's executed when I save an entry). This would then trigger the rebuild of your next site and your content would be updated once PM2 restarts your app process.

