I strongly agree to surrogate keys idea of @valse .

[Getting started with surrogate keys \| Fastly Help Guides](https://docs.fastly.com/en/guides/getting-started-with-surrogate-keys)

It's very simple and programmable feature to resource invalidate.

## cache invalidate usecase on SSG with fastly

- on deploy: invalidate all resources to regenerate page
- on update resource: invalidate that resource id (on postgresql, mysql and many ORM)

nikkei.com (Japasese major news site) uses this architecture.

## API thought experiment

for example.

```ts
// pages/[itemId].tsx
export async function getStaticProps(ctx) {
  return {
    props: { ... },
    cacheSurrogateKeys: [ctx.params.itemId], // this is proposal
    fallback: true
  }
}

export default function(props) {
  return <>...</>
}
```

and it's update and invalidate.

```ts
// pages/api/updateItem.ts
import cache from "next/cache";
export default async ({req}) => {
  const item = find(req.body.itemId);
  await item.update(params);
  cache.invalidate(req.body.itemId); // best effort in background
  return {ok: true};
}
```

on CI/CD

```bash
# deploy.sh
next build
# ...
vercel cache-invalidate all
```

## But it's depend on fastly feature (as far as I know)

Fastly has fast cache invalidate (50~300ms) by gossip protocol. It's core feature of fastly and their competitiveness.

Amazon S3 and Cloudfront can not do this strategy (1~3m to purge). GCP Cloud Function has `s-maxage` for edge cache but they don't have invalidate.

