I think it seems a little strange to build an API-endpoint directly into Next itself for revalidation the way it has been suggested here. It seems that different backends and CMS-es would structure their data differently, and may require their own way of interacting with the revalidation mechanics.

If you were to allow users to programmatically revalidate pages, would it not be good enough to expose a `revalidate` function from a module, let's call it `next/revalidate`. So that you could do:
``` javascript
import { revalidate } from 'next/revalidate';

revalidate('/path/to/page');
```

For an actual use case, you could build an API-route to handle revalidation for specific paths:
``` javascript
// /pages/api/revalidate.js
import { revalidate } from 'next/revalidate';

async function handler(req, res) {
  const { path } = req.query;
  await revalidate(path);
  res.end();
}

export default handler;
```

_Need authentication for the revalidate API?_ Add whatever authentication you want as a middleware.
_Need to group pages together with tags or something similar?_ Create the logic as a utility and use it in the API-route.

This may also allow developers to create packages for specific use-cases, such as a CMS that can trigger a webhook when a record is updated. But also any other ways to handle data, such as allowing manual triggers from an admin panel or something similar for systems that don't depend on a CMS.

Incremental Static Regeneration has been released in [Next.js 9.5](https://nextjs.org/blog/next-9-5#stable-incremental-static-regeneration) 🥳 

The implementation didn't change and it works just as it worked during its beta phase. To leverage Incremental static Regeneration first update Next.js to the latest version:

```bash
npm install next@latest

# or with yarn

yarn install next@latest
```

Then add `revalidate` to a page that's using `getStaticProps`:

```jsx
export async function getStaticProps() {
  return {
    props: await getDataFromCMS(),
    // we will attempt to re-generate the page:
    // - when a request comes in
    // - at most once every second
    revalidate: 1
  }
}
```

That's it! 🎉 

**Note:** If you were using Incremental Static Regeneration during the beta don't forget to rename `unstable_revalidate` to `revalidate` .

